Our work to date is up live on http://rightsim.org/RIGHT. The website still has issues, but the simmer code has been working incredibly well.

One thing we would like is to have feedback on time to completion or where timewise a simulation is for user feedback. Has anyone done this with simmer and shiny? Is it possible?

From ua5b521959296c79e at groups.google.com  Wed May 31 18:02:50 2017
From: ua5b521959296c79e at groups.google.com (Mark)
Date: Wed, 31 May 2017 18:02:50 +0000
Subject: Re: Shiny App Feedback
Message-ID: <jVazKnYJKXw._T_Hw7BhCgAJ@simmer-devel.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <jVazKnYJKXw.EEGvQkEEAAAJ@simmer-devel.groups.google.com>
References: <jVazKnYJKXw.EEGvQkEEAAAJ@simmer-devel.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/simmer-devel/c/jVazKnYJKXw/m/_T_Hw7BhCgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The easiest thing that comes to mind would be to just do a pilot run of a small number of replications, calculate a mean and variance and then calculate the 95th quantile as your predicted run-time.
I haven't looked, but I wouldn't be surprised if the progress bar in shiny allows you to provide an estimated completion time.
If you have a small number of parameters that can be changed and your analysis is static, you could also just create a surrogate model of the average/stdev runtime and use that to estimate your runtime for the selected params.  A GP/krigging model would probably be sufficient.

